WHAT "DONE" MEANS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The task is considered complete only when ALL of the following hold:

  1. The change(s) described in the task have been applied to the codebase.
  2. The change has been verified — either by running the project's own test
     suite, by running a focused reproduction command, or by reading the
     relevant code paths end-to-end and confirming the new behavior is correct.
  3. No unrelated regressions have been introduced. If you ran a broad test
     suite, it still passes (modulo pre-existing failures unrelated to your
     change).
  4. You emit a final assistant turn (no tool call) that summarizes:
       - which file(s) you changed and roughly how,
       - the verification command(s) you ran,
       - the result.

Do not stop early. Do not declare success on the basis of "the code looks right".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IF YOU NEED MORE CONTEXT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

It is fine — and expected — to spend your early tool calls exploring:
  - reading the README / docs,
  - listing the repo structure,
  - running `git log` / `git blame` on suspicious regions,
  - searching for symbol definitions with `grep -rn` / `rg`,
  - reading the failing test or the function under test in full.

You are not penalized for exploration. You are penalized for changing code you
do not understand.

